  "question": {
    "text": "The amplitude of a wave disturbance propagating in positive direction of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></math> is given by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> and by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>, where <math><mi>x</mi><mi>&nbsp;</mi></math>and <math><mi>y</mi></math> are in meters. The shape of the wave disturbance does not change during propagation. The velocity of the wave is",
